Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {PM10 concentrations in Europe have been declining in the last decades.
   An analysis of the PM10 concentrations and speciation measured at an
   urban and a regional background sites in NE Spain (BCN and MSY,
   respectively) is carried out with data from studies since 1999. Since
   1999, the average PM10 concentrations in BCN went from 49 to 20 mu g
   m(-3) (-1.4 {[}-2.3, -0.29] mu g m(-3) y(-1), p < 0.05). Having met the
   EU PM10 standards in 2010, mild actions were implemented to abate PM10,
   with local and regional efforts focusing on NO2. In 2020-2021, PM10 was
   composed by 25 \% of mineral matter (MM, 11 \% desert dust +14 \% urban
   dust), 22 \% organic matter, 20 \% secondary inorganic aerosols (SIA), 3
   \% elemental carbon, and 8 \% sea salt (SS). Moreover, 19 \% of PM10 has
   a natural origin, being a relevant contribution for compliance of the
   PM10 limit value (20 mu g m(-3)) of the Air Quality Directive (EU)
   2024/2881. Receptor modelling for 2020-2021 identified 10 major sources
   contributing to PM10: aged sea salt, mineral and vehicle emissions
   accounted for 81 \% of the PM10. This study is the first in Barcelona
   not being able to identify a shipping profile. Despite substantial
   reduction of direct shipping emissions, moored cruise ships are
   identified as its primary PM10 contributor. Overall, the results provide
   relevant information on the impact of policy actions implemented, and on
   identifying major target sources for the abatement of PM in urban
   environments of the W-Mediterranean.},
